 Shell India and several other cases of issue of shares at an alleged discount occurred, it is not clear whether such discount would be regarded as income. It needs to be noted that, even prior to 1st June 2010, since section 2(24) starts with "Income includes…." and not "Income means…..", items which are not included in that sub-section could also be liable to tax as income, if so regarded. There are several perspectives on the captioned matter. From one viewpoint, the issue of shares at a discount results in a benefit to the allottee. Such benefit can either be on capital or revenue account. While the benefit arose from issue of share capital, the purpose could be for the business operations i.e. increasing profits/reducing losses, of the Company. In such a case, while the form of the transaction was capital, the purpose was revenue in nature. Likewise, where the consideration for services or any other transaction (e.g. purchases) on revenue account is discharged by the issue of shares at a discount, the discount can be taxed as a benefit in lieu of income or tantamount to income. It may thus be argued that as a consideration for the non-resident parent to invest additional funds in the loss making Indian subsidiary, the latter issued its shares at a discount to the former. Continuing this viewpoint, consequently, discount on shares issued by an Indian subsidiary to an overseas parent to fund its business operations, is a benefit received by the non-resident parent, taxable in India under section 9(1) as arising from a capital asset or business connection situated in India. The Indian subsidiary may then be called upon to discharge the tax liability of the parent as its representative assessee under section 160/163 of the ITA, on the benefit by way of discount that the parent received. If this view is taken, based on the discount amount being revenue in nature, then the entire discount amount becomes taxable in the hands of the non-resident parent. This is similar to the case of issue of ESOP's to employees where the difference between the exercise price and the FMV of the ESOP shares is taxable as a perquisite under the specific provisions of section 17(2)(vi). There is a South African case relating to taxing the issue of shares at a discount in Ochberg v. CIR (1931) 5 SATC 93, which could have some relevance.

However, if the discount on share capital issued for funding business operations is regarded as being on capital account and not taxable, there could be a case to treat on revenue account, the income by way of interest forgone on the funds not collected due to issuing the shares at a discount, and make a transfer pricing adjustment directly on the Indian subsidiary in respect of such interest computed at an appropriate rate [Commissioner SARS v. Brummeria Renaissance (Pty.) Ltd [2007] (69 SATC 205)]. There are several counterpoints to this view however viz.

i.

The taxman cannot expect or determine that the Indian subsidiary would utilize the additional funds in a manner that would be most beneficial to the taxman instead of to itself. The taxman cannot compute or deem the income as accruing from the additional funds being deployed in non-business, high yielding securities, when the Indian subsidiary's business requirements would necessitate it deploying those additional funds in its operations for reducing its losses (which cannot be treated as income) or for expanding/integrating its production capacity (which does not result in any income due to its lengthy gestation period);

ii.

When shares are issued at a discount, the amount of discount can never be collected from the shareholders again in respect of those shares issued i.e. the discount amount is irretrievably forgone. Consequently, interest would have to be charged in perpetuity on the discount amount which would exceed even the entire discount amount and be absurd;

 of the Act gives the definition of a "short-term capital asset", and this definition becomes important, as it forms the basis for understanding the distinction between short-term capital gain and long-term capital gain. The general rule prescribed under this sub-section is that a capital asset will be treated as a short-term capital asset if it is held by an assessee for not more than 36 months immediately preceding the date of its transfer. However, an exception is carved out in the case of the following assets : ■ Shares held in a company;

■ Any other security listed in a recognised stock exchange in India;

■ Units of the Unit Trust of India;

■ Units of specified Mutual Funds.

In the case of these assets, the holding period of 36 months referred to above stands reduced to 12 months. Thus, if the specified securities are transferred before the expiry of 12 months from the date of their acquisition, the resulting capital gain will be treated as short-term capital gain. If the specified securities are transferred after the expiry of 12 months from the date of their acquisition, the resulting capital gain will be treated as long-term capital gain. This holding period of 12 months must be reckoned from the date of acquisition to the date of transfer on the "calendar month" basis and not on the "Gregorian month" basis, i.e., on a date to date" basis. For example, if the securities had been acquired on 11-6-1993, the period of 12 months will end on 10-6-1994. Since the period is to be reckoned with reference to the "holding" of the securities, it is not necessary that the securities must be listed in a stock exchange even at the time of acquisition. The condition of "listing" must be satisfied only at the time of transfer. In other words, the holding period will not be reckoned from the date of listing in case that date is later than the date of acquisition. Thus, if a security is allotted on, say, 15-5-1994, but was actually listed in a stock exchange on 1-6-1994, the holding period will be reckoned from 15-5-1994 and not from 1-6-1994.

Computation of the period of holding

7. Normally speaking, the computation of the period of holding in the case of securities other than their shares in companies will present no difficulty, since the period will be reckoned from the date on which the FII became the owner of such securities to the date on which the FII ceased to be owner of such securities. In the case of shares, however, certain points will have to be kept in view. It is common knowledge that shares can be acquired in different ways. The most common way is to acquire it either by subscription to a public offer or by purchase in the share market. In such cases, the date of acquisition of shares will be the date on which the FII is registered as a shareholder in the company's books. When such shares are later on transferred by the FII, the period of holding will be reckoned from the date of acquisition as aforesaid to the date of transfer. Shares can also be acquired by the issue of bonus shares by companies as well as by the issue or right shares. When right shares are offered, the FII may either opt to acquire it or to renounce it in favour of another party. In all these situations, the date of acquisition for the purpose of computing the period of holding will be determined in the following manner:

Bonus shares: For determining the period of holding of bonus shares, the date of their acquisition will be the date on which they were issued and not the date on which the shareholder acquired the original shares - CIT v. Chunilal Khushaldas [1974] 93 ITR 369 (Guj.); Executive of the Will of Late Shri Manecklal Premchand v. CIT [1990] 48 Taxman 310 (Bom.).

Right shares : Under sub-clause (d) of clause (i ) of the Explanation to section 2(42A) of the Act, inserted by the Finance Act, 1994 with effect from 1-4-1995, the date of acquisition of right shares in cases where the shareholder exercises the right to subscribe to such shares will be the date of allotment of such shares. Where the shareholder renounces the right offer in favour of another person (the renouncee), and the renouncee subscribes to the rights issue, the date of acquisition of the shares in the case of the renouncee will be taken as the date of allotment of the shares to the renouncee.

