 Shell India and several other cases of issue of shares at an alleged discount occurred, it is not clear whether such discount would be regarded as income. It needs to be noted that, even prior to 1st June 2010, since section 2(24) starts with "Income includes…." and not "Income means…..", items which are not included in that sub-section could also be liable to tax as income, if so regarded. There are several perspectives on the captioned matter. From one viewpoint, the issue of shares at a discount results in a benefit to the allottee. Such benefit can either be on capital or revenue account. While the benefit arose from issue of share capital, the purpose could be for the business operations i.e. increasing profits/reducing losses, of the Company. In such a case, while the form of the transaction was capital, the purpose was revenue in nature. Likewise, where the consideration for services or any other transaction (e.g. purchases) on revenue account is discharged by the issue of shares at a discount, the discount can be taxed as a benefit in lieu of income or tantamount to income. It may thus be argued that as a consideration for the non-resident parent to invest additional funds in the loss making Indian subsidiary, the latter issued its shares at a discount to the former. Continuing this viewpoint, consequently, discount on shares issued by an Indian subsidiary to an overseas parent to fund its business operations, is a benefit received by the non-resident parent, taxable in India under section 9(1) as arising from a capital asset or business connection situated in India. The Indian subsidiary may then be called upon to discharge the tax liability of the parent as its representative assessee under section 160/163 of the ITA, on the benefit by way of discount that the parent received. If this view is taken, based on the discount amount being revenue in nature, then the entire discount amount becomes taxable in the hands of the non-resident parent. This is similar to the case of issue of ESOP's to employees where the difference between the exercise price and the FMV of the ESOP shares is taxable as a perquisite under the specific provisions of section 17(2)(vi). There is a South African case relating to taxing the issue of shares at a discount in Ochberg v. CIR (1931) 5 SATC 93, which could have some relevance.

However, if the discount on share capital issued for funding business operations is regarded as being on capital account and not taxable, there could be a case to treat on revenue account, the income by way of interest forgone on the funds not collected due to issuing the shares at a discount, and make a transfer pricing adjustment directly on the Indian subsidiary in respect of such interest computed at an appropriate rate [Commissioner SARS v. Brummeria Renaissance (Pty.) Ltd [2007] (69 SATC 205)]. There are several counterpoints to this view however viz.

i.

The taxman cannot expect or determine that the Indian subsidiary would utilize the additional funds in a manner that would be most beneficial to the taxman instead of to itself. The taxman cannot compute or deem the income as accruing from the additional funds being deployed in non-business, high yielding securities, when the Indian subsidiary's business requirements would necessitate it deploying those additional funds in its operations for reducing its losses (which cannot be treated as income) or for expanding/integrating its production capacity (which does not result in any income due to its lengthy gestation period);

ii.

When shares are issued at a discount, the amount of discount can never be collected from the shareholders again in respect of those shares issued i.e. the discount amount is irretrievably forgone. Consequently, interest would have to be charged in perpetuity on the discount amount which would exceed even the entire discount amount and be absurd;
